        </packagedElement>
        <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491290706_506203_95946" xmi:uuid="f6b0a1bf-7a2d-42a7-af63-672c9e25ec40" xmi:type="uml:Class">
          <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491339105_236385_131625" xmi:uuid="1b71ee89-933b-40e5-aa6a-7f047a60ff1d" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
            <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491290706_506203_95946"/>
            <body>A Part is an identification of definition of a part. It collects the definitional information of the versions of a part. A Part shall always be categorized as 'piece part', 'product', 'software', 'tool', or 'raw material' using a ClassSelect. &lt;note&gt; A Part does not represent an actual physical object of the real world. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; A Part can be either a single piece part, an assembly of arbitrary complexity, a raw material, or a tool. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt; In the context of cars, a Part can be the car as a whole, the assembly of the engine, the car body, a fender, the side window glass, grease, or a stamping die. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;example&gt; In the context of aeroplanes, a Part can be the airplane as a whole, the assembly of the engine, the body of the airplane, a fender, the side window glass, grease, or a stamping die. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;note&gt; A Part is identified by an organization. The definition of the domain of uniqueness and the mechanism for guaranteeing the uniqueness of Part id are outside the scope of this capability. &lt;/note&gt;</body>
          </ownedComment>
          <name>Part</name>
