        <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491291895_880430_97039" xmi:uuid="586a08c6-1b7a-4ebb-9389-441df4501ec5" xmi:type="uml:Class">
          <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491349790_993905_137210" xmi:uuid="ae6c919b-c47c-4420-9fa1-d4cc0ae29a7e" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
            <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491291895_880430_97039"/>
            <body>A Requirement is an essential characteristic, condition, or capability that shall be met or exceeded by a system or a component to satisfy a contract, standard, specification, or other formally imposed document. &lt;note&gt; The term 'requirement' is used here in the sense that term is used in systems engineering and similar industrial domains. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; There can be many versions of a Requirement through RequirementVersion. There can also be more than one domain-specific view of a given RequirementVersion (using the RequirementView entity). The Requirement entity is simply a placeholder for holding a unique requirement. Most associations and properties could be defined at the Requirement, RequirementVersion or RequirementView level - that is in the context of a domain. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt;A requirement can be identified as 'NOx emissions requirement', and uniquely identified as 'Req2'. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;note&gt; Systems engineering tools and organizations can use differing identification mechanisms. Multiple identifiers can be managed in Requirement using the id attribute.&lt;/note&gt;</body>
          </ownedComment>
          <name>Requirement</name>
