        <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491291047_420530_96279" xmi:uuid="ffe23cb4-7cc5-4dd2-ad44-3000fcededde" xmi:type="uml:Class">
          <ownedComment xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491342343_671353_133418" xmi:uuid="89988d71-9b1b-4302-a32d-f428fd4fa15b" xmi:type="uml:Comment">
            <annotatedElement xmi:idref="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491291047_420530_96279"/>
            <body>A ProductClass is a type of ProductConcept that represents a set of similar products to be offered to the market. ProductClass objects that are related to each other by a ProductClassRelationship do not inherit any characteristics from each other. &lt;note&gt; The definition of product classes is driven by market and customer requirements and forecasting. A ProductClass often corresponds to the highest-level item(s) manufactured by an organization for customers. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt; In an organization which manufactures cars and engines for cars, the car models will be represented by ProductClass objects. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;note&gt; A ProductClass enables to represent customer-oriented identification of products that are to be delivered to customers, while a Part enables to identify and to track the history of items that are designed and manufactured, as a tangible solution, or component of the solution, for a ProductClass. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; A ProductClass can be characterized by a set of features or specifications identified by the customers or derived from customers' needs. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;note&gt; Depending on the kind of industry and products, a ProductClass might be offered to the customers in one single or in many different configurations. &lt;/note&gt;&lt;example&gt; The following figure depicts a detail of an example of a structure that is built up by ProductClass and ProductClassRelationship objects. &lt;/example&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;example_product_class_hierarchy.gif&lt;/name&gt; Example structure from the automotive domain of ProductClass objects in a hierarchy&lt;/figure&gt;</body>
          </ownedComment>
          <name>ProductClass</name>
          <generalization xmi:id="_18_4_1_65b021e_1677491293992_178997_98709" xmi:uuid="00cad91d-8eda-409c-8461-5e25399e8dd4" xmi:type="uml:Generalization">
